---
import Article from '../layouts/Article.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

// Compare models, never firms (rebuild spec §5.2).
const rows = [
  {
    dim: 'Who pays them',
    rep: 'The owner, exclusively. No compensation from any other party on the project.',
    pm: 'The builder. The PM is the builder’s employee, and their obligation runs to their employer.',
    ob: 'The builder. Open books are a reporting feature of the builder’s own service.',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Whose records you see',
    rep: 'An independent file built for you: verification reports, draw approvals, decision log — yours to keep.',
    pm: 'The builder’s records, summarized for you at the builder’s discretion.',
    ob: 'The builder’s records, in full. Genuine visibility — into what the builder recorded.',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Who verifies completion',
    rep: 'A third party with no stake in the answer, against work in place, before payment.',
    pm: 'The party requesting payment certifies its own progress.',
    ob: 'Still the builder. Open books document the claim; they do not check it.',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Who administers the holdback',
    rep: 'Administered on the owner’s side — calculation, lien-period tracking, documented release.',
    pm: 'It is the owner’s statutory obligation; the party being held back from cannot discharge it.',
    ob: 'Same. Better bookkeeping does not transfer a statutory obligation that sits with the owner.',
  },
  {
    dim: 'What happens in a dispute',
    rep: 'You hold an independent record: verified draws, documented decisions, a file built to stand up.',
    pm: 'The builder’s team represents the builder. You assemble your position afterwards, from their records.',
    ob: 'Visibility helps — but the record was authored by your counterparty throughout.',
  },
  {
    dim: 'What it costs',
    rep: 'A visible, separate fee — published structure, quoted per engagement.',
    pm: 'Included in the builder’s price — which means priced into it, not free.',
    ob: 'Usually included or a small premium; some of it is marketing.',
  },
  {
    dim: 'When it’s the right choice',
    rep: 'Significant projects, remote owners, complex sites, or wherever the money at stake justifies independent eyes.',
    pm: 'Always — a good builder-side PM makes every project better. It is necessary, not sufficient.',
    ob: 'Always worth having if offered. It is a good builder practice, not owner-side protection.',
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Isn’t the builder’s project manager already managing the project?',
    a: 'Yes, and a good one is worth a great deal. But they are the builder’s employee: when your interest and the builder’s diverge — an ambiguous scope item, a change order, a delay claim — their obligation runs to their employer. Builder-side management is necessary and not sufficient; it is not representation of the owner.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does open-book accounting replace independent verification?',
    a: 'No. Open books are a genuine improvement — full visibility into the builder’s records. But they remain the builder’s own record of the builder’s own work. Verification is a third party confirming the work exists before payment is released. Transparency shows you the claim; verification checks it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Who is responsible for the builders lien holdback?',
    a: 'The owner. In British Columbia the Builders Lien Act places the retention obligation on the party making payment, and it applies whether or not the contract mentions it. A builder cannot discharge it on your behalf — the holdback exists to protect the owner from the contracting chain, and the party being held back from cannot administer it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When is builder-provided management enough on its own?',
    a: 'Genuinely often: small scopes, a long-standing relationship with a builder you have direct experience of, an owner who is on site regularly, or a fixed-price contract with a performance bond. In those configurations, add a lawyer’s contract review before signing and you may not need more.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Article
  title={`Independent Representation vs Builder PM vs Open Books — ${SITE.name}`}
  heading="Independent representation, builder project management, and open books"
  kicker="An honest comparison"
  description="Three models for looking after an owner’s interests on a residential project — who pays each one, who verifies the work, who holds the holdback, and when each is genuinely the right choice."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>
    Three different things get called “looking after the owner” on a residential project: the builder’s own
    project management, open-book accounting offered by the builder, and independent owner’s representation.
    All three are real. They are not the same thing, and the differences are structural rather than a matter
    of quality. This page compares the models — not any firm — as fairly as we can manage, including the
    cases where the answer is that you don’t need us.
  </p>

  <div
    class="not-prose mt-10 overflow-x-auto xl:w-[68rem]"
    tabindex="0"
    role="region"
    aria-label="Comparison of the three models"
  >
    <table class="w-full min-w-[820px] border-collapse text-left">
      <caption class="annotation mb-4 text-left">
        Models compared, not firms. Each column describes the structure at its best.
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b-2 border-ink align-bottom">
          <th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[0.75rem] font-medium tracking-[0.08em] uppercase text-mid"></th>
          <th scope="col" class="w-[28%] py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[0.75rem] font-medium tracking-[0.08em] uppercase text-accent">
            Independent representation
          </th>
          <th scope="col" class="w-[28%] py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[0.75rem] font-medium tracking-[0.08em] uppercase text-mid">
            Builder project management
          </th>
          <th scope="col" class="w-[28%] py-3 font-mono text-[0.75rem] font-medium tracking-[0.08em] uppercase text-mid">
            Open-book accounting
          </th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map((row) => (
            <tr class="border-b border-rule align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-5 pr-4 text-sm font-semibold">
                {row.dim}
              </th>
              <td class="py-5 pr-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">{row.rep}</td>
              <td class="py-5 pr-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink/85">{row.pm}</td>
              <td class="py-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink/85">{row.ob}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The one distinction that decides the rest</h2>
  <p>
    Every row above reduces to a single question: <strong>is the party checking the work independent of the
    party being paid for it?</strong> Builder project management and open books can be excellent — run by
    conscientious people, worth having, better than the industry norm. They still sit on one side of the
    table. However good the bookkeeping, the party requesting payment is describing what the payment is for.
    Verification is different in kind: someone with no stake in the answer, confirming work in place before
    money moves, and writing it down. That is the whole of the difference, and no amount of quality on either
    side erases it.
  </p>

  <h2>When builder-provided management is enough</h2>
  <p>
    Often, genuinely. If the scope is modest and you can see the site regularly, the builder’s own management
    plus your own attention covers most of what representation would. If you have a long-standing
    relationship with a builder whose work you have direct experience of — not references, experience — the
    trust you have built is worth real money, and formalizing oversight may add little. A fixed-price
    contract with a performance bond moves much of the risk representation exists to watch. And an owner who
    is on site daily is already doing continuous verification, whether they call it that or not.
  </p>
  <p>
    In those configurations, spend a fraction of a representative’s fee on a construction lawyer’s review of
    the contract before signing, take the open books if they are offered, and proceed. We say this in
    writing because a comparison page that can’t name the cases against its author isn’t a comparison — and
    because <a href="/insights/do-i-need-one">telling people when they don’t need us</a> is the cheapest
    credibility we have.
  </p>

  <h2>Common questions</h2>
  <div class="not-prose mt-6 max-w-prose">
    {
      faqs.map((f) => (
        <details class="group border-t border-rule py-4">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer text-base font-semibold marker:text-accent">{f.q}</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed text-ink/90">{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <p class="mt-10">
    Where your project sits among these models is usually clear after one conversation.
    <a href="/project-review">Request a project review</a> — no fee, and our honest view either way.
  </p>
</Article>
